I write my postings with vim(), cut and paste them
from and then back into a browser text widgit (whose
editing functionalities are too trivial even to
consider using) for MailGate or Google Groups
posting.

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the text between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

Say the cursor is at (in vim()'s model, just to the
left of) the first "t" of the word "text" in the
second line of the above paragraph. Then the
following examples from among  vim()'s rich set of
cursor motion commands have the indicated encryption
effects, and applying rot13 a second time would of
course decrypt the text.

g?l (one character right)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the gext between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?3l (three characters right)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkt between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?12h (twelve characters left)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rbg13 bs gur text between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?k   (one line up, which includes the _whole_
      current line, an example that some vim()
      motion commands are done in "line mode"
      instead of "character mode", mostly to the
      surprise and annoyance of the unsuspecting
      user, who was naively expecting a very
      different result!)

Gur ivz() pbzznaq "t?" sbyybjrq ol n phefbe zbgvba
pbzznaq qbrf n ebg13 bs gur grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?$ (to the end of the current line)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?0 (to the beginning of the current line)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
pbzznaq qbrf n ebg13 bs gur text between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?e (to the end of one word following the cursor)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkg between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?3b (to the beginning of the third word preceeding
     the cursor)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a ebg13 bs gur text between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?) (to the first end of a sentence following the
    cursor)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
bevtva naq gur phefbe qrfgvangvba sbe gung pbzznaq.

g?{ (to the first beginning of a paragraph
    preceeding the cursor)

Gur ivz() pbzznaq "t?" sbyybjrq ol n phefbe zbgvba
pbzznaq qbrf n ebg13 bs gur text between the cursor
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?6w (to before the beginning of the sixth word
     following the word in which the cursor
     currently "sits")

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
bevtva naq the cursor destination for that command.

g?/man followed by an "enter" (to before the first
       occurance of the string "man")

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
command does a rot13 of the grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
bevtva naq gur phefbe qrfgvangvba sbe gung pbzmand.

g?? ("to" before the first line below the current
    line (a total surprise to _me_); done in "line"
    mode, so includes the _whole_ current line)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
pbzznaq qbrf n ebg13 bs gur grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
origin and the cursor destination for that command.

g?2? ("to" before the second line below the current
     line)

The vim() command "g?" followed by a cursor motion
pbzznaq qbrf n ebg13 bs gur grkg orgjrra gur phefbe
bevtva naq gur phefbe qrfgvangvba sbe gung pbzznaq.

That's already lots more vim() navigation commands
than I use in a normal week of typing, but you sort
of get the idea that having an editor first written
for secretaries means it's easy (or "confusing and
complicated as hell", your call) to "name" cursor
destinations in a document. A certain competing
editor may stuff that level of terseness in its
elisp and smoke it.

I'd still _much_ prefer that people use white space
rather than rot13 for spoilers, or just admit that
rgrn is "spoiler city" and put them into the message
undefended, though; like lots of others, my news
client does not, itself, do rot13 at all, and the
encrypted text is just intensely annoying to
encounter when I don't already have an editor window
open for vim().

